{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":0,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D. 9 inches","reason":"The median formula gives 3.5^2=(2·4^2+2·7^2-QR^2)/4. Thus QR^2=130-49=81 and QR=9.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1964_AMC_12_Q38","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"median-length-identity","label":"Use the median special case","methodDirectNodeIds":{"median-length-identity":["math:stewart-theorem"]},"nodeIds":["math:stewart-theorem"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_113","cnm_082","cnm_174","cnm_075"],"id":"symmetric-distance-sum","label":"Add squared distances from symmetric endpoints","methodDirectNodeIds":{"symmetric-distance-sum":["math:stewart-theorem","cnm_h173","cnm_148"]},"nodeIds":["math:stewart-theorem","cnm_h173","cnm_148","cnm_113","cnm_082","cnm_174","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":0,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 2√31","reason":"Writing a=BC, the median identity gives 4AM²=2AB²+2AC²-a². Thus a²=2·4²+2·8²-4·3²=124, so BC=2√31.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1975_AMC_12_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"median-length-identity","label":"Median length identity","methodDirectNodeIds":{"median-length-identity":["math:stewart-theorem"]},"nodeIds":["math:stewart-theorem"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_113","cnm_055","cnm_075"],"id":"pythagorean-cancellation","label":"Add two Pythagorean equations","methodDirectNodeIds":{"pythagorean-cancellation":["cnm_084","cnm_174","cnm_148","math:stewart-theorem"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_084","cnm_174","cnm_148","math:stewart-theorem","cnm_082","cnm_113","cnm_055","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: √2","reason":"Let BC = a, so the median also has length a. The median identity gives 4a² = 2(1²+2²)-a², hence 5a² = 10 and a = √2. This satisfies the triangle bounds 1<a<3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2002_AMC_12B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"median-length-identity","label":"Use the median-length identity","methodDirectNodeIds":{"median-length-identity":["math:stewart-theorem","cnm_220"]},"nodeIds":["math:stewart-theorem","cnm_220"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_113","cnm_055","cnm_075","cnm_111"],"id":"derive-median-relation","label":"Derive the median relation with coordinates","methodDirectNodeIds":{"derive-median-relation":["math:stewart-theorem","cnm_h173","cnm_148","cnm_220"]},"nodeIds":["math:stewart-theorem","cnm_h173","cnm_148","cnm_220","cnm_082","cnm_113","cnm_055","cnm_075","cnm_111"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":0,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 2a+b","reason":"For X interior to BC, put t=BX/BC. Stewart's identity gives AX²=(1-t)AB²+tAC²-t(1-t)BC²<max(AB²,AC²). Therefore AA'<b, BB'<a, and CC'<a, so s<2a+b.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1964_AMC_12_Q39","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"stewart-cevian-bound","label":"Stewart's weighted-square bound","methodDirectNodeIds":{"stewart-cevian-bound":["math:stewart-theorem"]},"nodeIds":["math:stewart-theorem"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":0,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: sqrt(166)","reason":"In triangle AOB, cos(AOB)=(8^2+4^2-6^2)/(2*8*4)=11/16. Therefore cos(AOD)=-11/16, giving AD^2=8^2+6^2-2*8*6*(-11/16)=166.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["The length OC=3 is not needed for either listed route.","OC = 3 is unnecessary for either listed route."],"questionId":"1967_AMC_12_Q32","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"stewart-on-triangle-abd","label":"Apply Stewart with the matched side weights","methodDirectNodeIds":{"stewart-on-triangle-abd":["math:stewart-theorem"]},"nodeIds":["math:stewart-theorem"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, infinitely many points.","reason":"Put the diameter endpoints at (-1,0) and (1,0). The squared-distance sum is 2(x^2+y^2)+2, so the condition gives x^2+y^2=1/2. This entire circle lies strictly inside the unit circle and contains infinitely many points.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1969_AMC_12_Q28","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"median-length-identity","label":"Use the median identity","methodDirectNodeIds":{"median-length-identity":["math:stewart-theorem"]},"nodeIds":["math:stewart-theorem"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 3√5/5","reason":"Place the right-angle vertex at (0,0) and the others at (a,0),(0,b). The trisection-point distances have squared sum 5(a²+b²)/9=5h²/9. Their stated lengths give this sum as sin²x+cos²x=1, so h=3/√5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1980_AMC_12_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"stewart-for-two-cevians","label":"Stewart's theorem for both cevians","methodDirectNodeIds":{"stewart-for-two-cevians":["math:stewart-theorem"]},"nodeIds":["math:stewart-theorem"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_113","cnm_148","cnm_h062","cnm_075"],"id":"midpoint-square-identity","label":"Use the midpoint of the hypotenuse","methodDirectNodeIds":{"midpoint-square-identity":["cnm_187","math:stewart-theorem","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_187","math:stewart-theorem","cnm_174","cnm_113","cnm_148","cnm_h062","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (2√10)","reason":"The angle-bisector theorem gives AB/AE = 2/3 and AD/AC = 1/2. Put AB = 2x, AE = 3x, AD = y, and AC = 2y. Stewart in ABE and ADC gives y² = 6x² - 6 and 2y² = 9x² + 18, so x² = 10. Thus the three sides are 2√10, 11, and 6√6; the shortest is 2√10.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1981_AMC_12_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"bisectors-and-stewart","label":"Angle bisectors and Stewart","methodDirectNodeIds":{"bisectors-and-stewart":["math:stewart-theorem"]},"nodeIds":["math:stewart-theorem"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 35","reason":"Choose D on AB with ∠ACD=∠A. Angle sums give AD=CD and BD=BC=27, hence AD=CD=21. Stewart's theorem gives 27b²+27²×21=48(21²+21×27), so b²=1225 and b=35.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1985_AMC_12_Q28","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"isosceles-cevian-stewart","label":"Isosceles construction and Stewart","methodDirectNodeIds":{"isosceles-cevian-stewart":["cnm_138","math:stewart-theorem"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_138","math:stewart-theorem"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B. 26","reason":"Let OX=x and OY=y. Then x^2+y^2/4=19^2 and x^2/4+y^2=22^2. Adding gives (5/4)(x^2+y^2)=845, so XY^2=x^2+y^2=676 and XY=26.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2002_AMC_10B_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"median-length-identities","label":"Use the median-length formula","methodDirectNodeIds":{"median-length-identities":["math:stewart-theorem"]},"nodeIds":["math:stewart-theorem"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 2√13","reason":"For legs u and v, the two squared median lengths are u²+v²/4 and v²+u²/4. Their sum is 25+40=65=(5/4)(u²+v²). Thus the hypotenuse squared is 52, giving 2√13.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1951_AMC_12_Q49","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"median-length-identities","label":"Use median-length identities","methodDirectNodeIds":{"median-length-identities":["math:stewart-theorem","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["math:stewart-theorem","cnm_174"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, AB=2√13","reason":"Let AC=a and BC=b. Then AD²=a²+b²/4=49 and BE²=a²/4+b²=16. Adding gives 5(a²+b²)/4=65, so AB²=52 and AB=2√13.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1958_AMC_12_Q43","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"median-length-identities","label":"Use median-length identities","methodDirectNodeIds":{"median-length-identities":["math:stewart-theorem","cnm_090"]},"nodeIds":["math:stewart-theorem","cnm_090"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 15","reason":"The semiperimeter is 39. Heron's formula gives area sqrt(39*12*13*14)=78sqrt(14), so the inradius is 2sqrt(14). Also cos B=(27^2+25^2-26^2)/(2*27*25)=113/225, hence sin(B/2)=2sqrt(14)/15. Therefore BI=r/sin(B/2)=15.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2012_AMC_12A_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"divide-the-angle-bisector","label":"Angle-bisector ratios and Stewart's theorem","methodDirectNodeIds":{"divide-the-angle-bisector":["math:angle-bisector-side-ratio","math:stewart-theorem"]},"nodeIds":["math:angle-bisector-side-ratio","math:stewart-theorem"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 6/7","reason":"Put J=(0,0), A=(2,0), O=(3,0), B=(5,0), and K=(6,0). For radius r and P=(x,y), tangency gives PA=r+2, PB=r+1, PO=3-r. Subtracting squared distance equations gives x=5r and x=6-2r. Thus 7r=6, so r=6/7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_12A_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"stewart-on-centers","label":"Stewart with collinear centers","methodDirectNodeIds":{"stewart-on-centers":["math:stewart-theorem","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["math:stewart-theorem","cnm_042"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, √22","reason":"Let X be the second intersection of line BD with the circle. Then BX=AB²/BC=12 and DX=12-6=6. The chord product at D gives r²-OD²=DC·DX=18, so r²=22 and r=√22.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1976_AMC_12_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"tangent-radius-and-median","label":"Tangent radius and median identity","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-radius-and-median":["cnm_262","cnm_174","math:stewart-theorem"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_262","cnm_174","math:stewart-theorem"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. 2","reason":"The cosine rule gives BC²=3²+6²−2·3·6 cos120°=63. The bisector theorem gives CD:DB=1:2, so CD·DB=14. Stewart's relation then gives AD²=3·6−14=4, hence AD=2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1983_AMC_12_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"general-cosine-bisector-and-stewart","label":"Use the 120-degree cosine, bisector ratio, and Stewart","methodDirectNodeIds":{"general-cosine-bisector-and-stewart":["math:general-cosine-law-application","cnm_h065","math:angle-bisector-side-ratio","math:stewart-theorem"]},"nodeIds":["math:general-cosine-law-application","cnm_h065","math:angle-bisector-side-ratio","math:stewart-theorem"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 17/6","reason":"Since 6² + 8² = 10², ABC is right at A, and its hypotenuse median AD is 5. Its area is 24, so both subtriangles have area 12. Their semiperimeters are 8 and 9, giving radii 12/8 and 12/9, whose sum is 17/6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_10B_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"median-and-heron","label":"Median formula and Heron","methodDirectNodeIds":{"median-and-heron":["math:stewart-theorem","math:heron-formula"]},"nodeIds":["math:stewart-theorem","math:heron-formula"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 12√2","reason":"AP = 5 and PC = 10. Stewart's theorem gives 13²·10+14²·5 = 15(BP²+50), so BP² = 128 and BP = 8√2. Similarity gives fourth-vertex distances BP/2 and 2BP from P on the same ray. Their separation is (2-1/2)BP = 12√2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_12B_Q11","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"similarity-and-stewart","label":"Similarity and a cevian length","methodDirectNodeIds":{"similarity-and-stewart":["cnm_271","cnm_272","math:stewart-theorem"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_271","cnm_272","math:stewart-theorem"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D (1565)","reason":"Let u=cos∠ABC. Opposite angles are supplementary, so the cosine laws give AC²=625-336u=625+600u. Hence u=0 and AC=25, making the radius 25/2. The quadrilateral area is 7·24/2+15·20/2=234. The remaining area is (625π-936)/4, and 625+936+4=1565.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_10A_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"median-perpendicular-bisectors","label":"Use equal medians to locate the center","methodDirectNodeIds":{"median-perpendicular-bisectors":["math:stewart-theorem","cnm_133","cnm_254","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["math:stewart-theorem","cnm_133","cnm_254","cnm_p148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 911","reason":"Doubling the median produces a triangle with sides 40, 42, and 2x, so 1 < x < 41. The area is at most 40·42/2 = 840, attained when AB is perpendicular to AC. Then x = √(40²+42²)/2 = 29. Therefore p+q+r+s = 1+41+840+29 = 911.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_12B_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"median-heron-quadratic","label":"Median formula and quadratic area square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"median-heron-quadratic":["math:stewart-theorem","math:general-cosine-law-application","math:heron-formula","cnm_235","cnm_111","cnm_195"]},"nodeIds":["math:stewart-theorem","math:heron-formula","cnm_235","math:general-cosine-law-application","cnm_111","cnm_195"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":9,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, the ratio of an altitude to its corresponding base","reason":"With a fixed base and height, the third vertex can slide parallel to the base while the angles change. The other data determine similarity: SAS or AA directly, reciprocal side ratios from altitudes, and side ratios from the median-square identities.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1951_AMC_12_Q29","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_270","cnm_112","cnm_113","cnm_174","cnm_148","cnm_082"],"id":"check-similarity-data","label":"Check which data determine similarity","methodDirectNodeIds":{"check-similarity-data":["cnm_271","cnm_p148","math:stewart-theorem","cnm_071"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_271","cnm_p148","math:stewart-theorem","cnm_071","cnm_270","cnm_112","cnm_113","cnm_174","cnm_148","cnm_082"]}]}],"layer":"ailleo","meaning":"Reviewed content-bound concept pra